## Nomos keeps synthetic warnings out of native tool errors

Source: Mark Gantlett · Show HN — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50337394
Published: 2026-11-02
Channel: hn

Nomos is a local harness that sits between a software repository and a coding agent. Its developer describes isolating tasks in Git worktrees and checking proposed changes before merging them. The first-party announcement also reports a narrower observability lesson: adding helpful warnings to tool results can make the underlying failure harder for an agent to interpret.

The author says an early hook, a handler that runs around tool use, injected process-failure and refusal warnings after operations completed. In a public benchmark, that synthetic text degraded attribution of native operating-system permission errors. The post reports a recovery after changing the hooks, but provides an author-run result rather than independent validation.

The revised approach first applies path guards before a tool runs. After execution, it keeps the hook silent on native result channels, preserving the original error evidence. A separate circuit breaker stops repeated identical failures. The broader harness also returns file-and-line fixes from verification gates. Those controls are distinct from the quality of the error record: the announcement does not establish that every agent shortcut is prevented.

Why it matters: For coding-agent harness authors: test whether hook messages preserve native failure evidence, separately from whether enforcement blocks an action.

