## EchoChain measures information changes across repeated model exchanges

Source: smusali · Show HN — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50331840
Published: 2026-11-02
Channel: hn

EchoChain is an open-source Python command-line tool for exploring how information changes when language models repeatedly exchange responses. Its author describes an AI version of telephone: a model converts an answer into a question, another answer follows, and the cycle repeats. The concern is whether factual details survive those transformations.

The announcement describes multi-model testing and model rotation, together with tracking factual claims, calculating semantic similarity and reporting fidelity scores. These are ways to examine changes across the sequence, rather than judging only its final answer. The author also lists reproducibility, statistical tests and visualizations as supported features. The post supplies no numerical comparison establishing which model retains information best.

The tool currently routes calls through OpenRouter and requires an API key for that service. The author connects the experiment to multi-agent systems in which generated information passes between agents without human validation. That is the motivation for the experiment, not evidence that a particular production system loses information at a measured rate. The announcement invites feedback on agent-building and evaluation frameworks.

Why it matters: For multi-agent evaluation teams: consider testing information retention at each handoff, while checking the tool’s scoring definitions before comparing models.

